## Part A — Chapter 1 snag list

### A1. Wildlife must never look like predation

Owner's report: the wren seemed to eat other animals. No creature is ever removed by another in the code. What it looks like:

- **Cause:** beetles go hidden inside their shelter while a wren perches on the same shelter, so the beetle appears to vanish next to the bird.
- **Cause:** the wren's journal text mentions searching for insects.

Fixes:

- [ ] Show that a hidden creature is resting inside its shelter (a small resting marker on the shelter, plus residents listed in its panel), so nothing appears to vanish.
- [ ] Birds perch beside the shelter, never on top of a resident's spot, and never at the moment a resident goes inside.
- [ ] Rewrite journal and description text so no species is described hunting or eating another animal. Check every fact against the no-predation rule.
- [ ] Add a content test that fails if facts mention hunting, eating or catching other animals.
- [ ] Record the no-predation rule in `CLAUDE.md` and `open-items.md` so future species follow it.

### A2. Seeds pile up with nowhere to use them

Owner's report: about 60 seeds by the end of the chapter, without many places to plant them.

- [ ] Slow seed production: longer intervals for wildflower seeds and other propagules, and a lower cap per plant.
- [ ] Stop producing a seed type once the player holds plenty of it (a soft inventory cap, for example 12), so seeds never pile up.
- [ ] Give seeds a use: a **seed bundle** recipe used as an ingredient in shelter upgrades (seed heads draw insects), and later as a bird-feeding feature in Chapter 2.
- [ ] Rebalance the playthrough test so its end-of-chapter inventory stays within sensible bounds.

### A3. A second upgrade tier for the first shelter

- [ ] Add a third level to the natural shelter in Chapter 1, for example **Mossy log lodge** (higher capacity and attraction, its own look).
  - It is reached after the shelter has had several residents.
  - It costs materials that include the new seed bundle.
- [ ] Chapter 2's branching choice (insect hotel, log lodge, nesting bundle) then becomes the fourth level.
- [ ] Tune wren arrival so the second tier is when birds become regular visitors.

### A4. A calmer interface: icons instead of wordy pop-ups

Owner's report: the interface gets too busy.

- [ ] Replace "Gathered …" and "Cleared overgrowth and found …" toasts with small **item icons** that rise from where the item was gathered, drift towards the Inventory tab, and fade out within about a second. Several items show as a short cluster with a count, for example "+2".
- [ ] Keep text only for milestones, shown as one compact line at a time, never stacked.
- [ ] No journal pop-ups at all. New entries and newly learned facts put a notification badge on the Journal tab, inviting the player to visit when they choose. Inside, new entries and facts are highlighted until read. This keeps the journal optional and intentional.
- [ ] The return recap mentions journal additions in a single line ("Your journal has 3 new notes") rather than listing them.
- [ ] Respect reduced motion: icons fade in place without travelling.

### A5. More shade in the first garden

Owner's report: few excellent spots make placement hard.

- [ ] Redesign the Chapter 1 map with more shaded and varied areas:
  - a third old tree, or a small stand of trees, near the pond
  - a shaded strip along the north wall
  - dense old shrubs that cast light shade
- [ ] Spread moss terrain so the shade-loving plants and the shelter have several good-to-excellent spots in different parts of the garden.
- [ ] Add a map test: at least N excellent tiles for each starter plant and for the shelter.
- [ ] Re-run the Chapter 1 playthrough test and adjust its tile choices.

### A6. The maple as a long-term goal

The maple taking hours to mature is good. Let it pay off in Chapter 2 rather than speeding it up:

- [ ] Keep the maple's growth roughly as is (about five hours, so it is mature the day after planting).
- [ ] Remind the player about it gently:
  - the inspect panel shows "fully grown in about N hours"
  - the return recap mentions the maple's progress
  - a Chapter 1 suggestion encourages planting it early
- [x] When mature, the maple yields **maple leaves**, a seasonal-looking material.
- [x] It also yields **maple sap**, gathered from the tree rather than processed by people. Its use: a **sap feeder**, a small dish on a post that draws a sap-feeding butterfly (for example a red admiral). Some real butterflies feed on tree sap rather than nectar, so the sap gives the maple an ecological role, not just a crafting one.
- [ ] Chapter 2 uses maple leaves in a reward:
  - an optional milestone, "A tree comes of age"
  - leaves as an ingredient in the nesting bundle branch and in a decorative maple-leaf lantern
- [ ] A mature maple attracts the Chapter 2 songbird a little more strongly, linking the two chapters.

